{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
Let us [G]pause in life's [C]pleasures
And [G]count its many tears,
While we [G]all sup sorrow [D]with the poor;
There's a [G]song that will [C]linger
For[G]ever in our ears;
Oh, [G]hard times [D]come again no [G]more.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
'Tis the [G]song, the [C]sigh of the [G]weary,
Hard times, hard times, [D]come again no [G]more.
Many [G]days you have [C]lingered
A[G]round my cabin [D]door;
Oh, [G]hard times [D]come again no [G]more.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
While we [G]seek mirth and [C]beauty
And [G]music light and gay,
There are [G]frail forms fainting [D]at the door;
Though their [G]voices are [C]silent,
Their [G]pleading looks will say,
Oh, [G]hard times [D]come again no [G]more.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
There's a [G]pale drooping [C]maiden
Who [G]toils her life away,
With a [G]worn heart whose better [D]days are o'er;
Though her [G]voice would be [C]merry,
'Tis [G]sighing all the day,
Oh, [G]hard times [D]come again no [G]more.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 4"}
'Tis a [G]sigh that is [C]wafted
A[G]cross the troubled wave,
'Tis a [G]wail that is heard up[D]on the shore,
'Tis a [G]dirge that is [C]murmured
A[G]round the lowly grave,
Oh, [G]hard times [D]come again no [G]more.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
{x_end: G}
